<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What this app will talk to, and which certificate it will accept.

    Pinning is declared here rather than built in code on purpose. A pin
    applied by the platform covers every request the app makes, including
    one written later by somebody who did not know there was a pinned
    socket factory to use. A pin applied in code covers the requests that
    remembered to use it.

    BEFORE THIS SHIPS, both pins below have to be replaced. They are
    placeholders and will refuse every connection, which is the right way
    for a placeholder to fail: the alternative is an app that looks
    pinned and is not.

        openssl s_client -connect sies.example.edu:443 -showcerts </dev/null \
          | openssl x509 -pubkey -noout \
          | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der \
          | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary \
          | openssl enc -base64

    TWO pins, and the second is not optional. A pin set with one entry is
    an app that stops working the day the certificate is replaced, in the
    middle of a term, with no way to fix it but a release. The second pin
    should be the public key of the backup certificate or of the issuing
    CA, something that will still be valid when the first is rotated.

    expiration is a deliberate safety catch: past that date the platform
    stops enforcing the pins rather than refusing every connection, so a
    build that outlives its pins degrades to ordinary certificate
    validation instead of bricking a professor's phone. Move it with each
    release.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <!--
        No cleartext anywhere, including on the development machine. An
        attendance record is a record about a named person, and a
        debug build that talks HTTP is how somebody ends up testing
        against production over a hotel network.

        For a local server during development, set
        sync_base_url to the https address of a tunnel, or add a
        debug-only override in app/src/debug/res/xml/.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">sies.example.edu</domain>

        <pin-set expiration="2027-06-30">
            <!-- REPLACE: the server's current public key. -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA=</pin>
            <!-- REPLACE: the backup key or the issuing CA. See above. -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
